# Barn swallow

The barn swallow is the most widespread swallow in the world, glossy blue above
with a long forked tail. It builds an open cup nest from mud pellets and stalks
of dry grass, plastered firmly to a rafter or a ledge inside a barn or under a
bridge, and lines it with feathers. The nest is fixed to its support and does
not move; a pair will repair the old one and use it again. Both adults feed the
brood on insects caught in flight. See [[hirundinidae]].
